$WW,1$$FG,5,0$Porting Lynx to ZealOS$FG$

$TR,"Overview"$
Lynx is a text-mode web browser that uses curses for its UI. To run
Lynx on ZealOS, we have two options:

1. Cross-compile Lynx on a Linux host and run it via our ELF loader
2. Rewrite key parts of Lynx in HolyC (native ZealOS)

This document covers both approaches.

$TR,"Cross-Compilation Approach"$

$FG,2$Step 1: Set up cross-compilation environment$FG$

On a Linux host with GCC installed:

$FG,4$
cd /path/to/lynx2.9.2

# Configure for static build
CFLAGS="-static" \
LDFLAGS="-static" \
./configure \
    --disable-nls \
    --disable-idna \
    --without-ssl \
    --with-screen=curses \
    --disable-color-style

make clean
make
$FG$

$FG,2$Step 2: Copy binary to ZealOS$FG$

Copy the resulting $FG,4$lynx$FG$ binary to:
$FG,4$/Home/POSIX/bin/lynx$FG$

$FG,2$Step 3: Run via ELF loader$FG$

In ZealOS:
$FG,4$
#include "/Home/POSIX/bin/MakeBin"
ELFRun("/Home/POSIX/bin/lynx", argc, argv);
$FG$

$TR,"Required libc Functions"$

Lynx requires these functions from our POSIX layer:

$FG,2$Already Implemented:$FG$
- String: strlen, strcpy, strncpy, strcmp, strncmp, strcat, strncat
- String: strchr, strrchr, strstr, strcasecmp, strncasecmp, strdup
- Memory: malloc, realloc, calloc, free, memcpy, memmove, memset
- I/O: fopen, fclose, fread, fwrite, fgets, fputs, printf, fprintf
- I/O: open, close, read, write, lseek, stat, fstat
- Directory: opendir, readdir, closedir, mkdir, rmdir, getcwd
- Process: fork, exec*, waitpid, getpid, system, popen
- Network: socket, connect, send, recv, gethostbyname, getaddrinfo
- Terminal: tcgetattr, tcsetattr, isatty
- Curses: initscr, endwin, newwin, addch, printw, getch, etc.
- Time: time, localtime, gmtime, mktime, strftime, gettimeofday

$FG,2$May Need Implementation:$FG$
- getpwuid, getpwnam (user database - partially done)
- sigsetjmp, siglongjmp (signal handling jumps)
- Some less common socket options

$TR,"ZealOS-Specific Adaptations"$

$FG,2$1. TLS/SSL$FG$

Lynx typically uses OpenSSL or GnuTLS. We have two options:

a) Disable SSL and use our native HTTPS:
   - Configure lynx with --without-ssl
   - Create a wrapper that intercepts https:// URLs
   - Route them through ZealOS HTTP/TLS stack

b) Create SSL shim library:
   - Implement enough of OpenSSL API
   - Map to our TLS implementation

$FG,2$2. Terminal Handling$FG$

Lynx expects ncurses. Our curses.ZC provides most functionality.
Key functions that may need adjustment:
- Color pairs (we support 8 colors)
- Special key handling (escape sequences)
- Mouse support (not currently implemented)

$FG,2$3. Configuration Files$FG$

Create these in /Home/POSIX/etc/:
- lynx.cfg    - Main configuration
- lynx.lss    - Color style sheet
- mailcap     - MIME handlers
- mime.types  - File type mappings

$TR,"Native HolyC Browser"$

Instead of porting Lynx, we could create a native HolyC web browser
using our existing HTTP/TLS stack. This would be:
- More integrated with ZealOS
- Use native graphics capabilities
- Not require POSIX compatibility layer for basic browsing

See /Home/Net/HTTP/Browser.ZC for a starting point.

$TR,"Missing libc Functions to Add"$

$FG,4$
// pwd.h - User database (partial)
struct passwd *getpwuid(uid_t uid);
struct passwd *getpwnam(char *name);
void setpwent(void);
void endpwent(void);
struct passwd *getpwent(void);

// grp.h - Group database
struct group *getgrgid(gid_t gid);
struct group *getgrnam(char *name);

// signal.h - Extended signals
int sigsetjmp(sigjmp_buf env, int savesigs);
void siglongjmp(sigjmp_buf env, int val);

// locale.h - Locale support
char *setlocale(int category, char *locale);
struct lconv *localeconv(void);
$FG$

$TR,"Build Instructions Summary"$

$FG,2$For Cross-Compiled Lynx:$FG$

1. On Linux:
   $FG,4$./configure --disable-nls --without-ssl && make$FG$

2. Copy lynx binary to ZealOS

3. In ZealOS:
   $FG,4$
   #include "/Home/POSIX/MakePOSIX"
   #include "/Home/POSIX/libc/MakeLibc"
   ELFRun("/Home/POSIX/bin/lynx", 1, {"lynx"});
   $FG$

$FG,2$For Native ZealOS Browser:$FG$

See /Home/Net/HTTP/MakeHTTP.ZC for the HTTP client.
A basic text browser can be built using:
- HTTPGet() for fetching pages
- Simple HTML parsing
- Native ZealOS text display or curses

